## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1 —
all present in `_references/gars-bio.lock.txt`; nothing is installed). The script takes both
input paths from `_system/resolve_artifact.py` at run time and only reads them.

1. **Resolve inputs.** Call `resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-a99c29f1 --assay
   rnaseq_bulk --type counts_gene` and `--type design`; abort if either is not `ok`.
2. **Load and align.** Read the counts TSV; set `gene_id` as the index and drop `gene_name`.
   Read the design CSV indexed by `sample_id`. Require that the count columns and the design
   rows are the same sample set (abort otherwise), and order the count columns to the design.
3. **Prepare counts.** Round the length-scaled counts to integers (they are salmon
   length-scaled TPM estimates, the input form DESeq2 accepts after rounding). Drop genes whose
   count is zero in all six samples. No other filtering.
4. **Variance-stabilising transform.** Build a `pydeseq2.dds.DeseqDataSet` with
   `design="~condition"`, then run `dds.vst(use_design=False)` — a *blind* VST, so the
   treatment labels play no part in the transformed values. Size factors use DESeq2's default
   median-of-ratios.
5. **Select genes.** Keep the 500 genes with the highest row variance of the VST values
   (the DESeq2 `plotPCA` default, `ntop=500`).
6. **PCA.** `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=6)` on the samples x 500-genes matrix,
   centred, not scaled. Record each sample's scores on all components and the fraction of
   variance explained per component.
7. **Plot.** Scatter PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   (treated / untreated), each point labelled with its `sample_id`; axis labels carry the
   percentage of variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (NN.N%)`). Saved as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
8. **Write tables.** Per-sample scores joined to the design columns; variance explained per
   component.

